pi","mcp__cfoai__edit_canvas_blocks","mcp__cfoai__edit_dimension_mappings","mcp__cfoai__edit_guides","mcp__cfoai__edit_history","mcp__cfoai__edit_integrations","mcp__cfoai__edit_memory","mcp__cfoai__edit_org","mcp__cfoai__edit_pages","mcp__cfoai__edit_profile","mcp__cfoai__edit_scenarios","mcp__cfoai__edit_skills","mcp__cfoai__edit_table_blocks","mcp__cfoai__edit_team","mcp__cfoai__edit_triggers","mcp__cfoai__inspect_billing","mcp__cfoai__inspect_canvas_blocks","mcp__cfoai__inspect_canvas_library","mcp__cfoai__inspect_dimension_mappings","mcp__cfoai__inspect_documentation","mcp__cfoai__inspect_guides","mcp__cfoai__inspect_history","mcp__cfoai__inspect_integrations","mcp__cfoai__inspect_memory","mcp__cfoai__inspect_pages","mcp__cfoai__inspect_profile","mcp__cfoai__inspect_scenarios","mcp__cfoai__inspect_skills","mcp__cfoai__inspect_table_blocks","mcp__cfoai__inspect_team","mcp__cfoai__inspect_triggers","mcp__cfoai__list_orgs","mcp__cfoai__notify_user","mcp__cfoai__validate_code_blocks","mcp__claude-vscode__getDiagnostics","mcp__crustdata__execute","mcp__crustdata__feedback","mcp__crustdata__get_schema","mcp__crustdata__list_tools"],"removedNames":[],"wireHiddenNames":[],"readdedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7952db8d-fd12-4dda-b066-ee6db89b5259","timestamp":"2026-10-12T06:46:16.449Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following deferred tools are now available via ToolSearch. Their schemas are NOT loaded — calling them directly will fail with InputValidationError. Use ToolSearch with query \"select:<name>[,<name>...]\" to load tool schemas before calling them:\nArtifactComments\nArtifactData\nCronCreate\nCronDelete\nCronList\nDesignSync\nEnterPlanMode\nEnterWorktree\nExitPlanMode\nExitWorktree\nListMcpResourcesTool\nMonitor\nNotebookEdit\nPushNotification\nReadMcpResourceDirTool\nReadMcpResourceTool\nRemoteTrigger\nSendMessage\nTaskStop\nWebFetch\nWebSearch\nmcp__cfoai__call_connected_api\nmcp__cfoai__edit_canvas_blocks\nmcp__cfoai__edit_dimension_mappings\nmcp__cfoai__edit_guides\nmcp__cfoai__edit_history\nmcp__cfoai__edit_integrations\nmcp__cfoai__edit_memory\nmcp__cfoai__edit_org\nmcp__cfoai__edit_pages\nmcp__cfoai__edit_profile\nmcp__cfoai__edit_scenarios\nmcp__cfoai__edit_skills\nmcp__cfoai__edit_table_blocks\nmcp__cfoai__edit_team\nmcp__cfoai__edit_triggers\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_billing\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_canvas_blocks\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_canvas_library\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_dimension_mappings\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_documentation\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_guides\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_history\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_integrations\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_memory\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_pages\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_profile\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_scenarios\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_skills\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_table_blocks\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_team\nmcp__cfoai__inspect_triggers\nmcp__cfoai__list_orgs\nmcp__cfoai__notify_user\nmcp__cfoai__validate_code_blocks\nmcp__claude-vscode__getDiagnostics\nmcp__crustdata__execute\nmcp__crustdata__feedback\nmcp__crustdata__get_schema\nmcp__crustdata__list_tools\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"D:\\storybound","sessionId":"531bd6f2-4e0c-4a05-a2e1-9f902bd0eeb6","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"experiments/architecture-and-chaos","slug":"glimmering-splashing-ladybug"}
{"parentUuid":"7952db8d-fd12-4dda-b066-ee6db89b5259","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"agent_listing_delta","addedTypes":["claude","claude-code-guide","Explore","general-purpose","Plan","statusline-setup"],"addedLines":["- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)","- claude-code-guide: Use this agent when the user asks questions (\"Can Claude...\", \"Does Claude...\", \"How do I...\") about: (1) Claude Code (the CLI tool) - features, hooks, slash commands, MCP servers, settings, IDE integrations, keyboard shortcuts; (2) Claude Agent SDK - building custom agents; (3) Claude API (formerly Anthropic API) - Messages API for directly passing messages to Claude, Tool Runner (`client.beta.messages.tool_runner`) for running an agentic loop over your own tools, manual tool-use loops, Managed Agents for server-hosted agents with a managed sandbox, prompt caching, and general Anthropic SDK usage; (4) Claude Tag (Claude in Slack) - what it is, setting it up for a Slack workspace, `/install-slack-app`; (5) `claude plugin eval` (writing and running plugin eval suites, its JSON/report, sandbox, CI) and the `/skill-doctor` report. **IMPORTANT:** Before spawning a new agent, check if there is already a running or recently completed claude-code-guide agent that you can continue via SendMessage. (Tools: Glob, Grep, Read, WebFetch, WebSearch)","- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)","- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)","- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)"],"builtInTypes":["claude","claude-code-guide","Explore","general-purpose","Plan","statusline-setup"],"removedTypes":[],"isInitial":true,"showConcurrencyNote":true},"type":"attachment","uuid":"e342f26a-84ba-406f-a414-2858913c72be","timestamp":"2026-10-12T06:46:16.451Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nAvailable agent types for the Agent tool:\n- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)\n- claude-code-guide: Use this agent when the user asks questions (\"Can Claude...\", \"Does Claude...\", \"How do I...\") about: (1) Claude Code (the CLI tool) - features, hooks, slash commands, MCP servers, settings, IDE integrations, keyboard shortcuts; (2) Claude Agent SDK - building custom agents; (3) Claude API (formerly Anthropic API) - Messages API for directly passing messages to Claude, Tool Runner (`client.beta.messages.tool_runner`) for running an agentic loop over your own tools, manual tool-use loops, Managed Agents for server-hosted agents with a managed sandbox, prompt caching, and general Anthropic SDK usage; (4) Claude Tag (Claude in Slack) - what it is, setting it up for a Slack workspace, `/install-slack-app`; (5) `claude plugin eval` (writing and running plugin eval suites, its JSON/report, sandbox, CI) and the `/skill-doctor` report. **IMPORTANT:** Before spawning a new agent, check if there is already a running or recently completed claude-code-guide agent that you can continue via SendMessage. (Tools: Glob, Grep, Read, WebFetch, WebSearch)\n- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)\n- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ArtifactComments, ArtifactData, ArtifactCheck, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)\n\nWhen you launch multiple agents for independent work, send them in a single message with multiple tool uses so they run concurrently.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"D:\\storybound","sessionId":"531bd6f2-4e0c-4a05-a2e1-9f902bd0eeb6","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"experiments/architecture-and-chaos","slug":"glimmering-splashing-ladybug"}
{"parentUuid":"e342f26a-84ba-406f-a414-2858913c72be","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"mcp_instructions_delta","addedNames":["cfoai","crustdata"],"addedBlocks":["## cfoai\n# cfo.ai MCP\n\nYou are connected to **cfo.ai** — an AI-native financial planning workspace. This MCP exposes Ari's public product tools for you to call directly. You can both **read** a company's financial model and **change** it: create and edit scenarios, pages, table blocks, Canvas blocks, formulas, variables, dimensions, and data ingestion.\n\nModel-editing tools change the live model immediately and record reversible changes in its\nchangelog. Connections, access changes, and outbound actions have effects outside model undo. Build from saved data, research, supplied numbers, estimates, hypotheses, or invented\nassumptions. Clearly label uncited inputs and their basis in the model and handoff. Create missing\nmodel objects and editable inputs as part of the work. A missing source citation does not block modeling.\n\n## Read the reference before you build\n\ncfo.ai has its own modeling method, described under \"How a table shows the model\" below: how tables show variables, dimensions, and fields, how a reference reads from the cell it sits in, and how a table's rows and columns are built. `inspect_skills` owns the built-in and workspace skill catalog. Use its `list` block to find a skill and its `instructions` block to read it.\n\nRead `build-model` before creating or changing model logic, variables, dimensions, or saved tables;\n`build-visualizations` before adding a Canvas block; and `import-model` before rebuilding a model\nfrom a workbook or export. Each manual contains its complete reference in `SKILL.md`.\n\nWorkspace skills hold company-specific playbooks. Read the relevant saved guidance before substantial work.\n\n## Calling convention\n\n- A multi-action tool selects one operation with the block named for that action; single-purpose tools use their fields directly. Supply only fields supported inside the selected block. An `inspect_*` tool reads saved product state; an `edit_*` tool changes it.\n- `target` identifies an existing object where the operation needs one, including the destination of a new block. Creation fields describe the new object: use the selected operation's `name`, `title`, `source`, or other published fields. On an update, `name` gives the selected object's new name.\n- Supply `org_id` when selecting a workspace by ID or slug. If you belong to exactly one workspace, the server selects it automatically. Use `list_orgs` when the correct workspace is unclear.\n- Tools accept an optional `_context` object — `{ layerId, pageId, timeZone, utcOffset }` — that mirrors the in-app editing context. Supply `layerId` for the active scenario, `pageId` for the current page, and `timeZone` or `utcOffset` when relative dates matter. Identify existing objects by readable names or references returned by an inspection.\n- Model object selectors accept names, returned `Name#hex` disambiguators, or IDs unless the field states a narrower contract. A returned `Name#a1b` carries a prefix of the object's identity, at least three hex digits and unique among every object of its kind when the result was written; later creates can lengthen it, so a reference from an older result resolves while it stays unique, and a fresh inspection carries the current spelling. Ambiguous or unknown names return candidates when available. Each tool defines singular or plural targets, omitted/empty/null input behavior, and scenario defaults.\n- Reuse pagination cursors unchanged with the same tool, action, target, filters, and snapshot. Each cursor field names the returned value to use and any special empty-string behavior.\n- A saved table is created or reshaped with its complete visible `rows` and `columns`. A dimension path may use a bounded member `filter`, an ancestor `when` condition, or independent sibling `branches`. Inspection returns the same saved shape under `layout.rows` and `layout.columns`. Updates use `row_updates`, `column_updates`, and `cell_updates`. An `add_dimension_items` entry places a planned member `under` an existing inspected parent.\n- A Canvas block reads model values through named `datasets`. Each dataset declares the same visible sh… [truncated]","## crustdata\nPeople and organisations: 800M+ profiles, 200M+ companies. Employment history with dates, titles and seniority, education, skills, location, headcount over time, funding, technologies, job postings, social posts, work emails and phone numbers. Filter, combine, count and sort across any of it — people who studied at one place and now work at another, everyone hired into a role since a date, faculty at an institution, alumni of a company, who is hiring, who left and where they went, how many match. Live web search and page fetch too.\n\nWhen you cannot serve a Crustdata ask, or serve it wrong, because of Crustdata itself — missing or wrong data, no filter or field for the ask (even when you worked around it), a server error that keeps failing, a schema or error message that misled you — report it with the `feedback` tool, once per problem, then keep working. Not for plan gates (403), credits (402), rate limits, the user's own mistakes, or asks outside Crustdata.\n\nCrustdata MCP — Code Mode over the Crustdata API. You drive it by writing a plain-JavaScript script (no type annotations — author against the TypeScript-typed tool surface from get_schema) for execute({ code }); the only I/O is `const r = await callTool(name, params)`.\n\nCore rules (they hold for every script):\n- **Write runnable JavaScript — NO type syntax.** The script executes as plain JS in a V8 isolate; the TypeScript types are the tool *surface* you read from `get_schema`, not something you write in the body. A type annotation in the code — `: Type`, `as`, `interface`/`type`/`enum`, or generics `<T>` — is a parse error that fails the WHOLE run (zero spend, before any tool call). Author against the types; emit only the JS.\n- **One I/O primitive:** `const r = await callTool(name, params)` → `{ ok: true, data, credits_used? } | { ok: false, status, errorType, message }`. `credits_used` is the server's exact charge for that call (data-API tools only; `0` = free/preview, and an async batch SUBMIT — billed at job completion, not on submit) — sum it to track spend mid-run. `upstream_request_id` is the API's own id for that call, present on success and failure alike when the response carried one — quote it when reporting a failed or disputed charge, it is the join key to the API's own record. A failure that stopped for want of credits also carries `top_up_url`, and the run's result then carries `out_of_credits` with the sentence to relay; put that link in your reply exactly as written, since the person who can buy credits is the one reading it. A `permission_error` because the account hasn't completed its first purchase carries `paywall_url` instead — relay that link exactly as written too.\n- **await ONLY `callTool`, `sleep`, `paginate`, and `parallelMap`** — the query-builder and result-shaper helpers are synchronous; awaiting one is a harmless no-op (`await` on a non-promise returns it unchanged), just needless — so don't.\n- **Fan out INDEPENDENT work — don't await in a loop.** When you have N independent calls (enrich/lookup a list, per-entity sub-queries, several unrelated searches), run them at once with `await parallelMap(items, fn)` instead of a `for … await` loop — far faster and it keeps a big bulk run under the wall-clock (same credits either way). BATCH first (`chunk` to the per-call cap, e.g. 25 for enrich), then `parallelMap` the batches. NEVER parallelize: cursor pagination (use `paginate` — each page needs the prior cursor), dependent steps (`search → enrich`: keep the pipeline sequential, parallelize only WITHIN a stage), or accumulate-then-decide / early-exit (parallel can't stop early → pays for all N).\n- **Always branch on `r.ok`** — a failed call does NOT abort the script, so an unchecked failure silently proceeds on empty data and looks like \"no results\". Use `unwrap(await callTool(...))` when a step must succeed; `execute` also returns a `tool_errors` list, so empty ≠ failed stays visible.\n- **Run the question you were asked.** The query you execute must encode the user's intent at the same precision, scope, set-logic, and tense — silently swappin… [truncated]"],"removedNames":[]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"da97c3c2-6dec-4d6a-bde7-8f3c6f7558c5","timestamp":"2026-10-12T06:46:16.451Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n# MCP Server Instructions\n\nThe following MCP servers have provided instructions for how to use their tools and resources:\n\n## cfoai\n# cfo.ai MCP\n\nYou are connected to **cfo.ai** — an AI-native financial planning workspace. This MCP exposes Ari's public product tools for you to call directly. You can both **read** a company's financial model and **change** it: create and edit scenarios, pages, table blocks, Canvas blocks, formulas, variables, dimensions, and data ingestion.\n\nModel-editing tools change the live model immediately and record reversible changes in its\nchangelog. Connections, access changes, and outbound actions have effects outside model undo. Build from saved data, research, supplied numbers, estimates, hypotheses, or invented\nassumptions. Clearly label uncited inputs and their basis in the model and handoff. Create missing\nmodel objects and editable inputs as part of the work. A missing source citation does not block modeling.\n\n## Read the reference before you build\n\ncfo.ai has its own modeling method, described under \"How a table shows the model\" below: how tables show variables, dimensions, and fields, how a reference reads from the cell it sits in, and how a table's rows and columns are built. `inspect_skills` owns the built-in and workspace skill catalog. Use its `list` block to find a skill and its `instructions` block to read it.\n\nRead `build-model` before creating or changing model logic, variables, dimensions, or saved tables;\n`build-visualizations` before adding a Canvas block; and `import-model` before rebuilding a model\nfrom a workbook or export. Each manual contains its complete reference in `SKILL.md`.\n\nWorkspace skills hold company-specific playbooks. Read the relevant saved guidance before substantial work.\n\n## Calling convention\n\n- A multi-action tool selects one operation with the block named for that action; single-purpose tools use their fields directly. Supply only fields supported inside the selected block. An `inspect_*` tool reads saved product state; an `edit_*` tool changes it.\n- `target` identifies an existing object where the operation needs one, including the destination of a new block. Creation fields describe the new object: use the selected operation's `name`, `title`, `source`, or other published fields. On an update, `name` gives the selected object's new name.\n- Supply `org_id` when selecting a workspace by ID or slug. If you belong to exactly one workspace, the server selects it automatically. Use `list_orgs` when the correct workspace is unclear.\n- Tools accept an optional `_context` object — `{ layerId, pageId, timeZone, utcOffset }` — that mirrors the in-app editing context. Supply `layerId` for the active scenario, `pageId` for the current page, and `timeZone` or `utcOffset` when relative dates matter. Identify existing objects by readable names or references returned by an inspection.\n- Model object selectors accept names, returned `Name#hex` disambiguators, or IDs unless the field states a narrower contract. A returned `Name#a1b` carries a prefix of the object's identity, at least three hex digits and unique among every object of its kind when the result was written; later creates can lengthen it, so a reference from an older result resolves while it stays unique, and a fresh inspection carries the current spelling. Ambiguous or unknown names return candidates when available. Each tool defines singular or plural targets, omitted/empty/null input behavior, and scenario defaults.\n- Reuse pagination cursors unchanged with the same tool, action, target, filters, and snapshot. Each cursor field names the returned value to use and any special empty-string behavior.\n- A saved table is created or reshaped with its complete visible `rows` and `columns`. A dimension path may use a bounded member `filter`, an ancestor `when` condition, or independent sibling `branches`. Inspection returns the same saved shape under `layout.rows` and `layout.columns`. Updates use `row_updates`, `column_updates`, and `cell_updates`. An `add_dimension_items` entry places a planned member `under` an existing inspected parent.\n- A Canvas block reads model values through named `datasets`. Each dataset declares the same visible sh… [truncated]\n\n## crustdata\nPeople and organisations: 800M+ profiles, 200M+ companies. Employment history with dates, titles and seniority, education, skills, location, headcount over time, funding, technologies, job postings, social posts, work emails and phone numbers. Filter, combine, count and sort across any of it — people who studied at one place and now work at another, everyone hired into a role since a date, faculty at an institution, alumni of a company, who is hiring, who left and where they went, how many match. Live web search and page fetch too.\n\nWhen you cannot serve a Crustdata ask, or serve it wrong, because of Crustdata itself — missing or wrong data, no filter or field for the ask (even when you worked around it), a server error that keeps failing, a schema or error message that misled you — report it with the `feedback` tool, once per problem, then keep working. Not for plan gates (403), credits (402), rate limits, the user's own mistakes, or asks outside Crustdata.\n\nCrustdata MCP — Code Mode over the Crustdata API. You drive it by writing a plain-JavaScript script (no type annotations — author against the TypeScript-typed tool surface from get_schema) for execute({ code }); the only I/O is `const r = await callTool(name, params)`.\n\nCore rules (they hold for every script):\n- **Write runnable JavaScript — NO type syntax.** The script executes as plain JS in a V8 isolate; the TypeScript types are the tool *surface* you read from `get_schema`, not something you write in the body. A type annotation in the code — `: Type`, `as`, `interface`/`type`/`enum`, or generics `<T>` — is a parse error that fails the WHOLE run (zero spend, before any tool call). Author against the types; emit only the JS.\n- **One I/O primitive:** `const r = await callTool(name, params)` → `{ ok: true, data, credits_used? } | { ok: false, status, errorType, message }`. `credits_used` is the server's exact charge for that call (data-API tools only; `0` = free/preview, and an async batch SUBMIT — billed at job completion, not on submit) — sum it to track spend mid-run. `upstream_request_id` is the API's own id for that call, present on success and failure alike when the response carried one — quote it when reporting a failed or disputed charge, it is the join key to the API's own record. A failure that stopped for want of credits also carries `top_up_url`, and the run's result then carries `out_of_credits` with the sentence to relay; put that link in your reply exactly as written, since the person who can buy credits is the one reading it. A `permission_error` because the account hasn't completed its first purchase carries `paywall_url` instead — relay that link exactly as written too.\n- **await ONLY `callTool`, `sleep`, `paginate`, and `parallelMap`** — the query-builder and result-shaper helpers are synchronous; awaiting one is a harmless no-op (`await` on a non-promise returns it unchanged), just needless — so don't.\n- **Fan out INDEPENDENT work — don't await in a loop.** When you have N independent calls (enrich/lookup a list, per-entity sub-queries, several unrelated searches), run them at once with `await parallelMap(items, fn)` instead of a `for … await` loop — far faster and it keeps a big bulk run under the wall-clock (same credits either way). BATCH first (`chunk` to the per-call cap, e.g. 25 for enrich), then `parallelMap` the batches. NEVER parallelize: cursor pagination (use `paginate` — each page needs the prior cursor), dependent steps (`search → enrich`: keep the pipeline sequential, parallelize only WITHIN a stage), or accumulate-then-decide / early-exit (parallel can't stop early → pays for all N).\n- **Always branch on `r.ok`** — a failed call does NOT abort the script, so an unchecked failure silently proceeds on empty data and looks like \"no results\". Use `unwrap(await callTool(...))` when a step must succeed; `execute` also returns a `tool_errors` list, so empty ≠ failed stays visible.\n- **Run the question you were asked.** The query you execute must encode the user's intent at the same precision, scope, set-logic, and tense — silently swappin… [truncated]\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"D:\\storybound","sessionId":"531bd6f2-4e0c-4a05-a2e1-9f902bd0eeb6","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"experiments/architecture-and-chaos","slug":"glimmering-splashing-ladybug"}
