{"parentUuid":"813a85b7-ced3-4c39-adc2-bf2cae0e7cf6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfwzYij4ru92B3SGquBUR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015PxyuShPMC9vzDiqHbw743","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"wc -c evidence/entire/20261012-IST-review-general-pr4.txt; head -c 6000 evidence/entire/20261012-IST-review-general-pr4.txt","description":"Read the Entire review of PR 4"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":875,"cache_read_input_tokens":226926,"output_tokens":147,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":875,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":147,"cache_read_input_tokens":226926,"cache_creation_input_tokens":875,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":875},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015PxyuShPMC9vzDiqHbw743":{"command":"cd /d/storybound && wc -c evidence/entire/20261012-IST-review-general-pr4.txt; head -c 6000 evidence/entire/20261012-IST-review-general-pr4.txt","description":"Read the Entire review of PR 4"}},"wireIngestContext":{"toolu_015PxyuShPMC9vzDiqHbw743":{"cwd":"/d/storybound"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"63a4d04f-b297-48a0-aeae-b4edf6010c5b","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfwzYi3diKfsQ2p5wBU14","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"16fafd36-edbb-497e-a295-cbea18972c77","timestamp":"2026-10-12T08:02:10.406Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"high","perTurnEffort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"D:\\storybound","sessionId":"531bd6f2-4e0c-4a05-a2e1-9f902bd0eeb6","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"experiments/architecture-and-chaos","slug":"glimmering-splashing-ladybug"}
{"parentUuid":"16fafd36-edbb-497e-a295-cbea18972c77","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"99a95678-24d8-4442-985d-24006c95eea8","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015PxyuShPMC9vzDiqHbw743","type":"tool_result","content":"6344 evidence/entire/20261012-IST-review-general-pr4.txt\nReviewing experiments/architecture-and-chaos vs origin/HEAD: 11 commits, 53 files changed, 1 uncommitted\nRunning review with claude-code...\n# claude-code review\n\nRequest changes. The branch has no quiz or games change; it adds failure drills, experiment tooling, a judge failover and tracing-context fixes. The reasons are below, and the main one is the safety-screen change that stops refusing drawings it could not screen. I did not run the tests.\n\n10 findings, most severe first:\n\n1. `src/storybound/pipeline.py:198`: Every 'screen_unavailable' result becomes a soft 'try again' error, but safety.screen() raises that reason for any exception, not only outages.\n\n   Failure scenario: In safety.py:79-84 a Gemini content-block on a disturbing drawing, a non-JSON or missing visual_risk answer, or a code bug all return reason_code 'screen_unavailable'. A drawing the screen could not clear now gets 'Pip is having trouble, try again in a minute' with outcome 'error' and no run.refused event. The child can resubmit the same image repeatedly, and the ledger and receipt record an outage instead of a refusal. The fix is to split the reason in safety.screen(): ClientError with retryable or 5xx status becomes 'screen_unavailable', while a block or unparseable answer stays a refusal.\n\n2. `src/storybound/oath/judge.py:158`: The ensemble lets one judge's 'pass' override the other judge's 'verification_error' on safety commitments.\n\n   Failure scenario: engine.gate() treats verification_error on a safety-class commitment as critical (fail closed). In ensemble_batch, if the second judge errors or returns an unverifiable verdict, `x.status == 'verification_error' and y.status != 'verification_error'` picks the other judge's pass. A safety check one judge could not run is therefore reported as passed, which contradicts the 'stricter verdict wins' claim in the docstring. The fix is to keep the verification_error for safety-class commitments, or only fall through to the other judge for non-safety ones.\n\n3. `src/storybound/oath/judge.py:132`: _RANK has no entry for 'warning', so a 'warning' ties with 'pass' and the result depends on argument order.\n\n   Failure scenario: Judge A returns pass and judge B returns warning. _RANK.get gives both 0, so `x if rank(x) >= rank(y)` picks A (pass) and drops B's warning, although the stricter verdict was meant to win. Add 'warning': 1 and shift the others up.\n\n4. `src/storybound/oath/judge.py:82`: The failover has no memory that the primary judge is down, so every batch pays the primary's full retry time before falling over, and a fallback row is written per batch.\n\n   Failure scenario: With the K2 judge down, each judge_batch call (one or more per page, per story) retries K2 to exhaustion before using claude-sonnet-4.6, which adds seconds per page. record_fallback also appends one entry per call, so the receipt shows dozens of identical 'judge' fallbacks. Cache a short-lived 'primary down' flag, or dedupe fallbacks per role.\n\n5. `src/storybound/pipeline.py:406`: n_stop decides 'the checkers were down' from `all(v.status == 'verification_error')`, which also matches causes unrelated to an outage.\n\n   Failure scenario: A deterministic verifier crash ('verifier crashed: ...') or a judge returning a violation without a verbatim quote yields verification_error on a safety commitment. If those are the only critical-gated verdicts, a page that may genuinely be unsafe ends as a friendly 'try again' error instead of the calm stop, and the ledger records an error. Distinguish a judge outage from the other verification_error causes, for example by a marker on the verdict, before choosing 'unavailable'.\n\n6. `src/storybound/config.py:61`: max_repair_attempts, the judge ensemble and the chaos hook are read from environment variables in product code, so a stray value silently changes behaviour.\n\n   Failure scenario: STORYBOUND_MAX_REPAIRS=0 left in .env turns off repairs for real children's stories. STORYBOUND_CHAOS in .env injects 503s and stalls into production calls. A non-integer value makes int() raise at import and breaks the whole app. The chaos module says 'never set in the product' but nothing enforces it. Gate these behind STORYBOUND_EXPERIMENT, or parse defensively and log loudly when they are active.\n\n7. `src/storybound/agents/writer.py:33`: normalise() unwraps any single-key dict, so the error message reports the wrong keys.\n\n   Failure scenario: A writer answer like {'correct': {...}} has one dict-valued key, so it is unwrapped to the inner {'text', 'feedback'}. assemble() then reports 'missing scenario; it had the keys [feedback, text]', which hides the real shape from whoever debugs it. Only unwrap when the lone key is a known wrapper ('page', 'scene'), as the docstring says, and print the keys of the original dict.\n\n8. `scripts/sweep.py:43`: audit_story judges after execute() has finished, outside the workflow span, and outside any metered() context.\n\n   Failure scenario: Audit judge calls create stand-alone guardrail spans that neatlogs exports as one-span fragment traces. These pollute the fragment-trace counts that HR-016 and count_fragment_traces.py report, and audit token cost is not metered. Run the audit inside the story's own trace and metered context, or tag the audit spans so they are excluded from the counts.\n\n9. `scripts/sweep.py:59`: one() now always passes pictures='auto' and media=media_on, which changes the configuration of every existing sweep.\n\n   Failure scenario: Earlier sweeps and baselines ran with the default pictures setting and media=False. Re-running them now takes the 'auto' picture-planning path, so a new run is not directly comparable with older ledger rows, and ledger rows use a different `pictures` value. Pass pictures='auto' only when --media is set.\n\n10. `scripts/count_fragment_traces.py:41`: `lo, hi = win[n]` raises KeyError when an experiment name is not in the ledger, aborting the whole run.\n\n    Failure scenario: Passing a misspelled name","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"0d6d9b58-ea49-4cc3-934c-4130c6a61089","timestamp":"2026-10-12T08:02:11.433Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"6344 evidence/entire/20261012-IST-review-general-pr4.txt\nReviewing experiments/architecture-and-chaos vs origin/HEAD: 11 commits, 53 files changed, 1 uncommitted\nRunning review with claude-code...\n# claude-code review\n\nRequest changes. The branch has no quiz or games change; it adds failure drills, experiment tooling, a judge failover and tracing-context fixes. The reasons are below, and the main one is the safety-screen change that stops refusing drawings it could not screen. I did not run the tests.\n\n10 findings, most severe first:\n\n1. `src/storybound/pipeline.py:198`: Every 'screen_unavailable' result becomes a soft 'try again' error, but safety.screen() raises that reason for any exception, not only outages.\n\n   Failure scenario: In safety.py:79-84 a Gemini content-block on a disturbing drawing, a non-JSON or missing visual_risk answer, or a code bug all return reason_code 'screen_unavailable'. A drawing the screen could not clear now gets 'Pip is having trouble, try again in a minute' with outcome 'error' and no run.refused event. The child can resubmit the same image repeatedly, and the ledger and receipt record an outage instead of a refusal. The fix is to split the reason in safety.screen(): ClientError with retryable or 5xx status becomes 'screen_unavailable', while a block or unparseable answer stays a refusal.\n\n2. `src/storybound/oath/judge.py:158`: The ensemble lets one judge's 'pass' override the other judge's 'verification_error' on safety commitments.\n\n   Failure scenario: engine.gate() treats verification_error on a safety-class commitment as critical (fail closed). In ensemble_batch, if the second judge errors or returns an unverifiable verdict, `x.status == 'verification_error' and y.status != 'verification_error'` picks the other judge's pass. A safety check one judge could not run is therefore reported as passed, which contradicts the 'stricter verdict wins' claim in the docstring. The fix is to keep the verification_error for safety-class commitments, or only fall through to the other judge for non-safety ones.\n\n3. `src/storybound/oath/judge.py:132`: _RANK has no entry for 'warning', so a 'warning' ties with 'pass' and the result depends on argument order.\n\n   Failure scenario: Judge A returns pass and judge B returns warning. _RANK.get gives both 0, so `x if rank(x) >= rank(y)` picks A (pass) and drops B's warning, although the stricter verdict was meant to win. Add 'warning': 1 and shift the others up.\n\n4. `src/storybound/oath/judge.py:82`: The failover has no memory that the primary judge is down, so every batch pays the primary's full retry time before falling over, and a fallback row is written per batch.\n\n   Failure scenario: With the K2 judge down, each judge_batch call (one or more per page, per story) retries K2 to exhaustion before using claude-sonnet-4.6, which adds seconds per page. record_fallback also appends one entry per call, so the receipt shows dozens of identical 'judge' fallbacks. Cache a short-lived 'primary down' flag, or dedupe fallbacks per role.\n\n5. `src/storybound/pipeline.py:406`: n_stop decides 'the checkers were down' from `all(v.status == 'verification_error')`, which also matches causes unrelated to an outage.\n\n   Failure scenario: A deterministic verifier crash ('verifier crashed: ...') or a judge returning a violation without a verbatim quote yields verification_error on a safety commitment. If those are the only critical-gated verdicts, a page that may genuinely be unsafe ends as a friendly 'try again' error instead of the calm stop, and the ledger records an error. Distinguish a judge outage from the other verification_error causes, for example by a marker on the verdict, before choosing 'unavailable'.\n\n6. `src/storybound/config.py:61`: max_repair_attempts, the judge ensemble and the chaos hook are read from environment variables in product code, so a stray value silently changes behaviour.\n\n   Failure scenario: STORYBOUND_MAX_REPAIRS=0 left in .env turns off repairs for real children's stories. STORYBOUND_CHAOS in .env injects 503s and stalls into production calls. A non-integer value makes int() raise at import and breaks the whole app. The chaos module says 'never set in the product' but nothing enforces it. Gate these behind STORYBOUND_EXPERIMENT, or parse defensively and log loudly when they are active.\n\n7. `src/storybound/agents/writer.py:33`: normalise() unwraps any single-key dict, so the error message reports the wrong keys.\n\n   Failure scenario: A writer answer like {'correct': {...}} has one dict-valued key, so it is unwrapped to the inner {'text', 'feedback'}. assemble() then reports 'missing scenario; it had the keys [feedback, text]', which hides the real shape from whoever debugs it. Only unwrap when the lone key is a known wrapper ('page', 'scene'), as the docstring says, and print the keys of the original dict.\n\n8. `scripts/sweep.py:43`: audit_story judges after execute() has finished, outside the workflow span, and outside any metered() context.\n\n   Failure scenario: Audit judge calls create stand-alone guardrail spans that neatlogs exports as one-span fragment traces. These pollute the fragment-trace counts that HR-016 and count_fragment_traces.py report, and audit token cost is not metered. Run the audit inside the story's own trace and metered context, or tag the audit spans so they are excluded from the counts.\n\n9. `scripts/sweep.py:59`: one() now always passes pictures='auto' and media=media_on, which changes the configuration of every existing sweep.\n\n   Failure scenario: Earlier sweeps and baselines ran with the default pictures setting and media=False. Re-running them now takes the 'auto' picture-planning path, so a new run is not directly comparable with older ledger rows, and ledger rows use a different `pictures` value. Pass pictures='auto' only when --media is set.\n\n10. `scripts/count_fragment_traces.py:41`: `lo, hi = win[n]` raises KeyError when an experiment name is not in the ledger, aborting the whole run.\n\n    Failure scenario: Passing a misspelled name","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"63a4d04f-b297-48a0-aeae-b4edf6010c5b","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/d/storybound","root":"/d/storybound","branch":"experiments/architecture-and-chaos","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":1},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"hatif03/storybound","visibility":"public"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"hatif03/storybound","visibility":"public"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"hatif03/storybound","visibility":"public"}]}},"live_cwd":"/d/storybound","platform":"windows"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"16fafd36-edbb-497e-a295-cbea18972c77","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"D:\\storybound","sessionId":"531bd6f2-4e0c-4a05-a2e1-9f902bd0eeb6","version":"2.1.296","gitBranch":"experiments/architecture-and-chaos","slug":"glimmering-splashing-ladybug"}
